### English summary

This repository publishes the %%N_ROWS%% World Heritage Irrigation Structures (WHIS) in Japan, recognized by ICID between 2014 and 2026, as Linked Open Data (Turtle, JSON-LD, GeoJSON and CSV), with source URLs for every record. It reuses schema.org, GeoSPARQL, W3C Basic Geo, DCMI Terms and DCAT, and defines a small `whis:` vocabulary only where needed (registration year, built year and its basis, facility type, water source, managing organization, irrigated area, representative point). Values that differ between official sources are left blank, and both values are recorded in a comment. Links to Wikidata (`owl:sameAs`) are added only after manual verification; components that exist in Wikidata are linked with `schema:hasPart`, and water sources are linked to Wikidata river and lake items. Data shapes are validated with SHACL on every build (GitHub Actions), and an in-browser SPARQL page (Oxigraph/WebAssembly) is provided. Photos from Wikimedia Commons (via Wikidata P18) are shown with their creators and licenses (`schema:ImageObject`), and external identifiers found in the linked Wikidata items (OpenStreetMap, GeoNames, NDL, Japanese cultural heritage databases, Japanese Wikipedia) are published as `rdfs:seeAlso` links. Facility types are modelled as a SKOS concept scheme mapped to FAO AGROVOC and Wikidata classes. The elevation of each representative point is derived from GSI DEM tiles (DEM1A, then DEM5A/5B/5C, then DEM10B) and cross-checked against the GSI elevation service. The map lets users switch to current aerial imagery and a colour relief map, and overlay historical aerial photographs (1945–1950, 1961–1969, 1974–1978) from GSI to compare land use around each structure. Linksets are described with VoID, and the landing page embeds a schema.org `Dataset` description. Data: CC BY 4.0 (photos keep their own licenses). Code: MIT.
